# Project: K1 Weekly Wonders — kids' learning game platform

Monorepo, two folders at workspace root:
- `games/` — Vite + React frontend (the games + teacher/admin tools)
- `server/` — Node/Express + Mongoose backend, deployed on Render

Do not re-explain React/Tailwind/Express basics. Only reason about
project-specific architecture below. If unsure whether a helper/model
already exists, check the relevant file listed here before writing a new one.

NOTE: this file describes the target architecture after the class-type /
admin-role overhaul (see IMPLEMENTATION_PROMPT.md if that work isn't done
yet). If code in the repo doesn't match a section here, treat the repo as
stale/mid-migration, not this file as wrong.

## Stack (games/)
react19, react-router-dom v7, zustand v5 (+persist), tailwindcss v4 via
`@tailwindcss/vite` (NOT the v3 postcss/config workflow — no
`tailwind.config.js`; theme/config lives in CSS via `@import "tailwindcss"`
and `@theme`), motion (framer-motion's new package name — `import { motion }
from 'motion/react'`), phaser v4, @dnd-kit/* (drag-reorder in the admin
panel), react-confetti, use-sound, react-helmet-async, clsx + tailwind-merge
(check for a shared `cn()` util before writing conditional classNames by
hand).

## Stack (server/)
Express + Mongoose (MongoDB). No JWT/session middleware — auth is a shared
"teacher code" checked against the DB per-request. See Auth model below.

## Core domain model
- **ClassType**: `'k1' | 'k2'` (enum, not a Mongo collection — same
  hardcoded-list pattern as `GAME_CATALOG`; add a third value the same way
  if needed later). Determines which game arrangement a class sees.
- **Class** (`ClassInfo`): `classId`, `className`, `image` (nullable,
  placeholder when absent), `classType`. Teacher list is *derived* by
  querying Teacher for matching classId, not stored on the class doc.
- **Teacher**: `code` (unique access code), `name`, `classId` (their home
  class), `role: 'teacher' | 'admin'`.
- **Student** (`Student.js`): `studentId` (server-generated UUID, not
  Mongo `_id`), `classId`, `fullName`, `nickname` (optional). classId-scoped,
  unaffected by classType. Pure roster data for now — not wired into game
  unlocking, PlaySession matching, or anything else. Don't add any
  functional linkage as part of the class-type overhaul; adding a student
  is a standalone action with no side effects elsewhere yet.
- **GameAccess**: scoped by **classType**, not classId: `unlocked`,
  `shiny` (featured), `order`, `added` (opted in from the catalogue), per
  `{classType, gameKey}` (unique compound index). Every class of the same
  type sees identical game arrangement/lock state.
- **Scheduled unlock**: `GameAccess.unlockAt` (Date, nullable) is the only new
  field the scheduling feature added. A row with `unlockAt` set is *always*
  `unlocked: false` — the schedule is a promise to flip it later, not a
  parallel state. Unlocking early clears `unlockAt` (the teacher just did what
  the schedule was going to do); removing a game or re-adding it also clears
  it. Due unlocks are applied **lazily on read** by `resolveDueUnlocks()`
  inside `getGameAccessRows()` — there is no cron, so a schedule still fires
  correctly after the server slept through the exact minute. Because of that,
  every read path that returns game rows auto-unlocks first; don't add a new
  read path that queries `GameAccess` directly and skips it.
- **PlaySession**: one row per completed round, still `classId`-scoped
  (`game`, `playerName`, `classId`, `stars`, `totalRounds`, `peakStreak`,
  optional `elapsedSeconds`/`mistakes` for time-trial games, `device`
  fingerprint).
- **Legacy default**: `classId: 'k12026-pny'`, `classType: 'k1'`
  ("Kindergarten 1"). Any classId-scoped fallback (missing-classId logic,
  model defaults) uses this id; classType defaults to `'k1'` when unset.

## Auth model — two tiers, both DB-validated server-side
- `isTeacher`/`isAdmin` in the zustand player store are UI state only, NOT
  a security boundary. Every write endpoint re-validates `teacherCode`
  against the DB.
- **Teacher** (`requireTeacher`): exactly three capabilities — view their
  own class's info (name/id/image/teacher list), add students to their
  own roster, and view (never edit) their own class type's game
  arrangement. No other mutation surface should be reachable by a
  non-admin anywhere in the panel — no reorder, lock/unlock, shiny, catalogue
  add/remove, student edit/delete, or class-info edit.
- **Admin** (`requireAdmin`, stricter than `requireTeacher` — role must be
  `'admin'`): the only role that can mutate `GameAccess` (lock/unlock,
  reorder, shiny, catalogue add/remove). Admins edit by `classType`, not by
  their own classId — they can manage K1 and/or K2 regardless of which
  class is their homeroom.
- Login: `NameGate.jsx` calls `POST /api/teacher-login` with the code and
  gets back `{name, classId, className, classType, role}` from the server
  — there is no client-side hardcoded teacher-code mirror anymore (it was
  removed; it caused new/edited teachers to be unable to log in since the
  frontend copy would drift from the DB).

## Frontend gating flow (unchanged by the classType overhaul)
`NameGate.jsx` (whole-app entry) → captures player name (legacy default
class) or teacher code (real class via the login endpoint) → persisted in
`playerStore.js`. `GameAccessGate.jsx` (wraps each game) → re-checks
per-game unlock via `gameAccess.js`'s `useIsGameUnlocked`; teachers/admins
always pass through. Both gates independently re-fetch if data isn't
loaded for the current classId. **This entire read/gating path is still
classId-based** — the server resolves `classId → classType` internally
for `GET /api/game-access`, so none of this frontend code needed to change
for the overhaul. New game routes still need both gates, in that order.

## Key frontend files
- `playerStore.js` — persisted zustand: playerName, classId, className,
  isTeacher, isAdmin, classType, teacherCode.
- `gameAccess.js` — `GAME_CATALOG` (canonical game list), the
  classId-based read store used by Home/GameAccessGate (unchanged), plus
  classType-scoped admin mutators (`setGameUnlockedForType`,
  `setGameShinyForType`, `setGameOrderForType`, `addGameToType`,
  `removeGameFromType`) used only by the admin panel.
- `logPlaySession.js` — called by every game on completion
  (`classId`-scoped, unaffected by classType). Also holds
  `fetchStats`/`fetchSummary`/`fetchAllPlays`/`deletePlayerGame`.
- `students.js` / `classInfo.js` — roster + class-info API, classId-scoped,
  available to any teacher for their own class.
- `main.jsx` — all game routes lazy-loaded. Phaser-based games live under
  `BonusGames/<name>/PhaserDemo.jsx` even when not labeled "bonus" in the
  catalog (e.g. Game4).
- `GameAccessPanel.jsx` — for admins, the tab bar is **"K1 Games" / "K2
  Games" / Students / Settings** (two separate top-level tabs, not a
  toggle inside one tab), each fully editable independently and defaulting
  to the admin's own classType on open. Non-admin teachers instead see a
  single **"Games"** tab (their own classType only), read-only — no drag/
  lock/shiny controls, just current state as static badges. Students tab
  is classId-scoped and unaffected by role either way.

## Server endpoint contract (server/server.js)
- `POST /api/teacher-login` — `{code}` → `{name, classId, className, classType, role}` or 401.
- `GET /api/classes` — list of `{id, name, classType}`.
- `GET /api/classes/:classId` — `{classId, className, image, classType, teachers[]}`.
- `GET /api/game-access?classId=` — READ, resolves classId→classType
  server-side, returns that type's rows. Contract unchanged from before
  the overhaul on purpose.
- `PUT/POST/DELETE /api/game-access[...]` — WRITE, admin-only
  (`requireAdmin`), body includes `classType` directly (not resolved from
  the caller's own classId).
- `PUT /api/game-access/:gameKey/schedule` — sets `unlockAt` (`{unlockAt}`
  ISO string). Rejects a past time (400) and an already-unlocked game
  (409). Must stay **above** `/api/game-access/:gameKey` in the file.
- `GET/POST/DELETE /api/students` — teacher-only (`requireTeacher`),
  scoped to the caller's own `classId`.
- `DELETE /api/classes/:classId/identities/:name` — deletes a name-only
  ("light") identity by removing its play sessions, since that history is the
  only thing that makes it exist. 409s if the name still matches a roster
  student (the UI must delete the student instead). Matches `classId` against
  both the URL param and the actor's own classId — the two aren't always the
  same string, which is why this one endpoint is defensive about it.
- `POST /api/plays` — logs a session; `classId`-scoped, falls back to the
  legacy class if absent client-side.
- `GET /api/stats`, `/api/summary`, `/api/plays` — teacher dashboard reads.
- `DELETE /api/plays` — teacher-only.
All routes return `{ error: '...' }` with non-2xx on failure; frontend
helpers throw `new Error(body.error || 'fallback message')`.

## Mongoose/Mongo gotchas
- Nested subdocument fields must avoid a key literally named `type`
  (Mongoose reads it as a type-declaration shorthand) — see
  `PlaySession.device.kind` (not `.type`).
- **Schema field changes don't migrate indexes.** When `GameAccess` moved
  its unique key from `{classId, gameKey}` to `{classType, gameKey}`, the
  old index had to be explicitly dropped and `syncIndexes()` called — a
  Mongoose schema edit alone does not do this in MongoDB.
- One-time data migrations run at boot (in the `mongoose.connect().then()`
  chain, before `app.listen`) and must be self-guarding/idempotent — check
  for the absence of the new field / presence of the old one before acting,
  so redeploys don't redo or corrupt the migration.

## Conventions to match when writing new code
- Comments explain *why*, not *what* — short rationale comments above
  non-obvious logic, not narration of obvious lines.
- Prefer editing/extending existing zustand stores and API helper files
  over creating parallel ones — one store per domain, one thin
  fetch-helper file per API concern.
- Env var: `VITE_API_BASE_URL` (frontend, defaults to
  `http://localhost:4000`). Backend uses `MONGODB_URI`.

## Scheduled unlocks / the "next game" banner
- `NextGameTimer.jsx` is **schedule-driven, not calendar-driven**. The old
  hardcoded "new game every Friday" countdown is gone — there is no default
  cadence anymore.
- It renders **nothing** when the class has no pending schedule; callers gate
  it on `useNextScheduledGame()` so the surrounding layout collapses cleanly
  (Home.jsx renders it conditionally; BetaHome's `TimerLeaderboardCard` also
  drops its divider). The Weekly Champions leaderboard always shows regardless.
- `nextScheduledGame(games)` (in `gameAccess.js`) picks the soonest
  `!unlocked && unlockAt` game. The banner names that game and counts down in
  days/hours/mins/secs, then calls `fetchGameAccess()` at zero so the server
  applies the unlock and the banner either rolls on or disappears.
- Teachers see it too (it's a schedule preview); the admin has no class, so it
  self-hides for them.
- Panel control: the clock button in each locked game row opens
  `ScheduleUnlockDialog`. Scheduled rows show an "⏰ Unlocks ..." badge. The
  button is hidden entirely once a game is unlocked.

## Adding a new game — checklist
1. Build the game component/scene (React or Phaser) under `games/src`.
2. Add a `lazy()` import + `<Route>` in `main.jsx`.
3. Add an entry to `GAME_CATALOG` in `gameAccess.js`.
4. Wrap the route content in `NameGate` → `GameAccessGate`.
5. Call `logPlaySession(...)` on completion with the matching `game` slug.
6. It appears once an **admin** adds it to a class type from the Catalogue
   tab (`addGameToType`) — teachers cannot do this themselves anymore.

## Adding a new class type (e.g. a future 'k3')
1. Add the value to the `classType` enum in `models/ClassInfo.js` and
   `models/GameAccess.js`.
2. Add it as a new tab (alongside "K1 Games" / "K2 Games") in
   `GameAccessPanel.jsx`'s admin tab bar.
3. No GameAccess rows exist for it until an admin adds games to it via the
   Catalogue tab — an empty class type is a valid, expected state.
